# XCU csplit
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The -k option is not tested with an error: an implementation may find the
# error before it has made any file, so nothing could be required to remain.
# A first line matching a first expression is avoided, since the resulting
# empty file is unspecified.

assertion  XCU.csplit.001
anchor     utilities/csplit.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/csplit.html#STDOUT
anchor     utilities/csplit.html#OUTPUT FILES
class      required
since      1992
says       csplit with line-number operands writes the lines before the first
           number to xx00, those up to the second number to xx01, and so on,
           and writes one line with each file's size in bytes.
test       t/001-line-numbers.sh

assertion  XCU.csplit.002
anchor     utilities/csplit.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       csplit /rexp/ ends the piece before the first following line that
           matches, moved by a +n or -n offset, and the next piece starts
           there.
test       t/002-regular-expression.sh

assertion  XCU.csplit.003
anchor     utilities/csplit.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       csplit %rexp% advances to the matching line without writing a file
           for the lines skipped.
test       t/003-skip-section.sh

assertion  XCU.csplit.004
anchor     utilities/csplit.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       A {num} operand after a regular expression applies it num more
           times, and after a line number splits every that many lines num
           more times.
test       t/004-repeat.sh

assertion  XCU.csplit.005
anchor     utilities/csplit.html#OPTIONS/-f
anchor     utilities/csplit.html#OPTIONS/-n
class      required
since      1992
says       csplit -f prefix names the files prefix00, prefix01, ..., and -n
           number gives the file numbers that many digits.
test       t/005-file-names.sh

assertion  XCU.csplit.006
anchor     utilities/csplit.html#OPTIONS/-s
class      required
since      1992
says       csplit -s writes no size lines to standard output.
test       t/006-quiet.sh

assertion  XCU.csplit.007
anchor     utilities/csplit.html#CONSEQUENCES OF ERRORS
anchor     utilities/csplit.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      1992
says       An operand that matches no line, or names a line past the end of
           the input, gives a nonzero status, and without -k no file made by
           that run remains.
test       t/007-error-removes-files.sh

assertion  XCU.csplit.008
anchor     utilities/csplit.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       A file operand of - means standard input.
test       t/008-standard-input.sh
